# CSS Tutorial Section Rule

When the user says **"apply to \<Section\>"** (or similar, e.g. `@css_tutorial.mdc apply to CSS Introduction`), document that tutorial section from the **open browser tab** (or the site they name, default [W3Schools CSS](https://www.w3schools.com/css/default.asp)).

They may also name a **range**: **"apply to \<Start\> to \<End\>"** (e.g. `apply to CSS Introduction to CSS Comments`). Process every left/sidebar item from Start through End **inclusive**, in nav order. One named section = one course file in `courses/`. A range = one course file per section in that span. Do **not** crawl the rest of the course, skip items in the range, or continue past End. If Start or End does not match a nav item, list nearby items and stop — do not guess. Finish each section (read → sandbox → run → snap → write course file → tick `tutorial_main.md`) before starting the next.

**Names mean the parent group, never the child landing page.** The user refers to the **parent** dropdown or H2 sidebar group when a name matches a group. **CSS Colors**, **CSS Backgrounds**, **CSS Borders**, **CSS Flexbox**, and other groups work this way. When a name matches both a parent and a child, **choose the parent**. If **Start** is a parent, begin at that group's first child. If **End** is a parent, finish at that group's last child. Still do **not** continue into the **next sibling group**. Skip **Code Challenge** and **\* Exercises** children unless the user names those pages. Trailing meta (Certificate, Editor, Exercises, Quiz, Practice, Syllabus, Study Plan, Interview, Compiler, Training) is **out of scope** unless named.

This output is a **detailed study tutorial**, not a thin recap. The reader must be able to learn the whole section from the course file alone — without ever opening W3Schools. That means **every idea explained in depth, every site example rebuilt and run, extra clarifying examples added where they help, and every result shown as a snap with a written description of what happened.** When in doubt, add more explanation and more examples, not less.

Match the grain and layout of `Personal/Files/javascript/courses/js_introduction.md`. You can view the index in `Personal/Files/css/tutorial_main.md`.

## Workflow Steps

1. **Read** `Personal/bin/format.md` once for **accordion spacing and Q&A nesting only**. Ignore its Lab and Code children — they are removed in this rule. This rule’s heading order and Detailed Explanation layout win.
2. **Open the section in the browser**
   - Default site: [https://www.w3schools.com/css/default.asp](https://www.w3schools.com/css/default.asp).
   - Use the existing Cursor browser tab if it is already on that tutorial.
   - If not, navigate to the site, then click the named item in the left/sidebar (or in-page heading). Example: **CSS Introduction**.
   - If the name does not match, list nearby nav items and stop — do not guess a different section.
   - Scroll the **full** section. Read every heading, note, example, exercise, table, and warning. Capture the **section URL**.
3. **Rebuild examples in the sandbox**
   - Create `Personal/Files/css/code_sandbox/<slug>/` (`CSS Introduction` → `css-introduction`).
   - Write runnable files. Prefer `index.html` plus `styles.css` when the Tryit is a page. One HTML file per Example. Extra `.js` only if the section uses it.
   - Recreate **every** Tryit / runnable example **and every reference-table row** on the page. Do not invent unrelated features, and do not drop examples or table rows to save space. You **may** add small extra variations when they make a concept clearer — label them as your own example.
   - If the section has **no** runnable example, skip the run/snap steps and say so in Detailed Explanation.
4. **Run the example**
   - Serve the sandbox over **http** (Cursor browser blocks `file://`). From `code_sandbox`, use a local static server, then open `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<slug>/`.
   - Confirm the output matches the tutorial example.
   - If the example **errors** or will not run, research a fix — prefer **official docs** ([MDN CSS](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS)). Tutorial sites often keep older snippets after the language, browsers, or tools have moved on. Use the current correct form in the sandbox, and note the mismatch in Detailed Explanation (what the page showed vs what works now). Do not invent unrelated features.
   - Stop the server when snaps are done.
5. **Snap source and result**
   - **Result:** screenshot the **running** sandbox page in the Cursor browser. That is the outcome the reader must see.
   - **Code:** snap the sandbox source. Prefer **CodeSnap** (`CodeSnap 📸` / `codesnap.start`) on the editor selection. If the agent cannot fire CodeSnap, screenshot the example as shown (tutorial editor or sandbox source) and still save a PNG.
   - Save PNGs under `Personal/Files/css/code_sandbox/snaps/`. Use **per-example numbering** so each example has its own pair:
     - `<slug>-01-code.png`, `<slug>-02-code.png`, …
     - `<slug>-01-result.png`, `<slug>-02-result.png`, …
     - A single-example section may use `<slug>-code.png` / `<slug>-result.png`.
   - Embed **relative** images from the course file: `../code_sandbox/snaps/<file>.png`
6. **Write** one course file per section to `Personal/Files/css/courses/<filename>.md`. **Never overwrite** an existing course file; if one with the same title already exists, ask before replacing it.
   - Filename = section title lowercased, punctuation dropped, spaces/`/`/`&` → underscores (`CSS Introduction` → `css_introduction.md`).
   - File starts with `# <Section Title>`, then `[Back to CSS Tutorial](../tutorial_main.md)`, then the study body from **Introduction** onward. **No outer** `<details>` wrapper around the whole section. Terminal Commands and Questions and Answers stay as **closed child accordions**.
7. **Tick the index.** In `Personal/Files/css/tutorial_main.md`, change the matching line from `- [ ] Title` to `- [x] Title [View](./courses/<filename>.md)`. If the title is missing, add it in nav order under the right `##` group. Do not remove other titles.

## Format Requirements

Child-accordion **spacing** matches `Personal/.cursor/rules/notes_doc.mdc` and `Personal/bin/format.md`. Heading **order and children** follow this rule:

- Course file `#` heading = the section title the user named (e.g. `CSS Introduction`).
- After the `#` heading, a back-link: `[Back to CSS Tutorial](../tutorial_main.md)`.
- **Accordion spacing** (Terminal Commands / Q&A / Answer only): one blank line after every `</summary>` and one blank line before every `</details>`.
- **Introduction**, **Detailed Explanation**, **Summary**, **References** = plain `##` headings (not child accordions).
- **Introduction must list every Example title.** After the short prose (what the section is), list **all** `### **Example N: Title**` headings in order — if the section has 34 examples, list all 34. Do not summarize, skip, or group them. Each line ends with a **`[View](#…)`** link that scrolls to that example. Use this shape (no code in the Introduction):

```markdown
This section has **N** examples:

- [x] **Example 1:** Title of example 1 [View](#css-introduction-example-01)
- [x] **Example 2:** Title of example 2 [View](#css-introduction-example-02)
- [x] **Example N:** Title of example N [View](#css-introduction-example-NN)
```

The titles must match the Detailed Explanation headings exactly. The **View** target is `#<slug>-example-NN` (slug from the section title: `CSS Introduction` → `css-introduction`; NN is two digits). Place `<a id="<slug>-example-NN"></a>` immediately before the matching `### **Example N:**` heading so the link scrolls there. This list is a table of contents so the reader can see the whole section at a glance.

- After Detailed Explanation, only these **closed child accordions**, in this order:
  1. **Terminal Commands**
  2. **Questions and Answers**
- **No `Lab` section.** **No separate `Code` section.** All prose, fenced code, code snaps, and result snaps live **inside Detailed Explanation**, next to the idea they demonstrate.
- Q&A: **8–15** questions that cover the actual examples (inputs, outputs, pitfalls), not just definitions; each answer is nested `<details><summary>Answer</summary>` with **only** `- [x]` lines.
- First References link = the **section URL** from the browser. Then other links from that page (and MDN CSS when you used them).
- If a snap file is missing, do not fake an `<img>` path. Finish the snaps first.

### Detailed Explanation (the merged study body)

This is the heart of the tutorial and absorbs what used to be the separate **Code** section. Write it as a **guided, example-driven walkthrough**. Start with a short `- [x]` concept list, then walk **every** example from the page in order (plus any extra variations you add). **For each example, always include all five parts, in this order:**

1. **Heading** — `<a id="<slug>-example-NN"></a>` then `### **Example N: Title**` (title from the page, or a clear label). The `id` is what Introduction `[View](#<slug>-example-NN)` scrolls to.
2. **Explanation** — `- [x]` lines describing **what it does, why it works, and what to notice** (edge cases, pitfalls, "do not" advice). Be generous: multiple bullets and nested sub-points, not one sentence. Explain any table, note, or warning here in the same style.
3. **Tested code** — a fenced block (`css / html`) with the **actual sandbox source that ran** (never an untested paste from the site). Mention the sandbox path, e.g. `Sandbox: code_sandbox/<slug>/index.html`.
4. **Code snap** — `<img ... src="../code_sandbox/snaps/<slug>-NN-code.png" />` right after the fence.
5. **Result snap + written outcome** — `<img ... src="../code_sandbox/snaps/<slug>-NN-result.png" />`, immediately followed by at least one `- [x]` line that **describes the outcome in words**. The image must never be the only explanation of the result.

Cover **all** examples this way — do not summarize several into one. If official docs required newer syntax than the page, keep **both**: the page’s idea and the working current form, and say how they differ. Prefer thoroughness: more examples and richer explanation is the goal.

### Grain: one named thing = one `### Example` (CSS Selectors is the model)

**CSS Selectors** is the required grain: the page names several constructs, and **each** gets its own heading with its own sandbox file, snaps, and outcome. Same idea as **JS Output** in the JavaScript tutorial.

**Never** list many methods / properties / keywords / selectors in a bullet and then show **one** representative snippet. Listing names is not teaching them.

Walk the page in order and emit a **separate** `### **Example N: Title**` (all five parts) for **each** of:

1. **Every Tryit / runnable example** on the page — including several Tryits of the *same* construct with different arguments.
2. **Every row of a reference table**. One row = one Example. Use the table’s Name as the heading.
3. **Every named construct** the page is teaching even if the page only shows a list — still build a small runnable demo.
4. **Every heading/section** that introduces a distinct idea (notes, warnings, “do not use”, exercises). Deprecated APIs still get an Example that **runs** them and says not to use them; do not skip the row.

**Reference / catalog pages (highest risk).** If the page is a catalog, you are not done until **every table row** has its own Example. You may **not**:

- Collapse the table into “useful names” / “see the site for the full list”
- Ship one sandbox that prints many results under a single Example heading
- Pick a “typical” item as a stand-in for the rest
- Group unrelated items into one fence unless they are a **single** Tryit on the page

**Sandbox layout:** one file per Example, plus a hub `index.html` when useful. Number snaps `<slug>-NN-code.png` / `<slug>-NN-result.png` in the **same order** as the Example headings. A single combined dump file is not a substitute.

**Q&A** must ask about **several different Examples**, not five generic definitions while many items went undemoed. Still 8–15 questions; spread them across the page.

